window.docContents[126].resolve({"/ISO-80000-3-___-Space-and-Time/Capturing-a-Remark___s-mathematics___-the-surface-element-of-an-area-_LPAR_item-3-3_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-3-___-Space-and-Time--Capturing-a-Remark___s-mathematics___-the-surface-element-of-an-area-_LPAR_item-3-3_RPAR_":{"contents":"Each ISO 80000 item carries a Remarks field, and those remarks often hold the\nquantity's defining mathematics. Item 3-3 (area) is the example: its remark gives the\nsurface element of a surface in terms of the Gaussian coordinates u, v and the\ndeterminant g of the metric tensor (ISO 80000-2). Rather than transcribe the licensed\ntext or reduce it to a dimensional annotation, this work formalizes the relation — as\nreal differential-geometric objects with proved properties — so the mathematics behind\nthe kind is itself checked.\n\n\n\nThe first fundamental form (the metric tensor of ISO 80000-2) at a point is the\n2 × 2 Gram matrix ⟪tᵢ, tⱼ⟫ of the surface's two tangent vectors. It is symmetric\nand positive-semidefinite, so its determinant g is non-negative.\n\nRealized as firstFundamentalForm t := Matrix.gram ℝ t over Mathlib's Gram-matrix\nlibrary; symmetry is isHermitian_gram and metricDet_nonneg is the determinant of a\npositive-semidefinite matrix.\n\nThe surface element dA = √g is the area-element density at a point, and the area of\na surface patch is its integral over the parameter region (A = ∬ √g du dv). Uses\nthe dimension map for the L² reading of the resulting kind.\n\nareaElement t := Real.sqrt (metricDet t) and surfaceArea t s := ∫ x in s,\nareaElement (t x). surfaceArea_const shows a uniformly-parametrized patch has area\nelement × (area of the parameter region).\n\nThe element squares back to the determinant: (dA)² = g. The √ in dA = √g is\nfaithful — the element is exactly the square root of the determinant of the metric\ntensor.\n\nareaElement_sq : areaElement t ^ 2 = metricDet t, from Real.sq_sqrt and the\nnon-negativity of g.\n\nA flat patch recovers its Euclidean area. For a surface lying in the plane the element\nequals |det A|, the change-of-variables area density of the parametrization — so the\n√g reading computes the actual area. This is the correctness anchor that pins the\nconvention the prose remark leaves implicit.\n\nareaElement_eq_abs_det : areaElement t = |(Matrix.of fun i j => t j i).det|, via\ngram_eq_conjTranspose_mul (so g = (det A)²) and Real.sqrt_sq_eq_abs.\n\nRegularity. The element is strictly positive exactly when the two tangent vectors are\nlinearly independent — a non-degenerate (regular) point. A vanishing element marks a\ndegenerate parametrization.\n\nareaElement_pos_iff : 0 < areaElement t ↔ LinearIndependent ℝ t, from Real.sqrt_pos\nand det_gram_ne_zero_iff_linearIndependent.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-3 — Space and Time","header":"22.5. Capturing a Remark's mathematics: the surface element of an area (item 3-3)","id":"/ISO-80000-3-___-Space-and-Time/Capturing-a-Remark___s-mathematics___-the-surface-element-of-an-area-_LPAR_item-3-3_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-3-___-Space-and-Time--Capturing-a-Remark___s-mathematics___-the-surface-element-of-an-area-_LPAR_item-3-3_RPAR_"},"/Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM/Quantities___-numbers___-and-the-count/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Quantities___-numbers___-and-the-count":{"contents":"* This work* Dybkær (2009)* ISO 80000-1:2022* VIM 3 (JCGM 200:2012)* VIM 4 2CD* Quantity* §13.3.3* 4.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* Carrier* §13.5 (extensive aggregation)* —* —* —* LawfulCarrier* no section given* —* —* —* instCarrierNat* no section given* 5 (dimension 1; counts not named)* 1.8 Note 4 (quantity of dimension one)* 1.10 quantity with unit one; 1.6 Note 4* Part9.numberOfEntities* no section given* 5 (dimension 1; counts not named)* 1.8 Note 4* 1.6 Note 4; 1.2 Note 3* Quantity.instLE* §12.16* 4.1 (comparable by order)* 1.26 ordinal quantity* 1.33 Note 2* Quantity.instDecidableLE* no section given* —* —* —* DifferenceKind* §12.19* —* 1.21 quantity calculus* 1.29 quantity calculus; 1.4 interval quantity\n\n* Agreement. The count is a quantity. VIM 3 1.8 Note 4 states that numbers of entities\nare quantities of dimension one, and VIM 4 2CD adds that number of entities can be\nregarded as a base quantity. This work carries a count in Nat (instCarrierNat), at\na ratio-scale kind, and its ISO 80000-9 catalogue entry numberOfEntities has\ndimension one. A count is therefore a Quantity k Nat of a declared kind and never a\nbare number: two counts of different kinds cannot be added or compared.* ISO 80000-1:2022 does not name the count. Clause 5 treats quantities whose\ndimensional exponents are all zero, notes that there is no agreed name for them, and\nsays that such quantities are dimensionally simply numbers. It does not mention\nnumbers of entities. Here a dimension-one quantity keeps its kind, so it is never\nmerely a number.* Quantity k R is narrower than quantity. In ISO 80000-1 and the VIM a quantity is\na property, and the word covers both the general and the individual quantity. A\nQuantity k R is a magnitude of one fixed kind k, held in a number type R. It has\nno bearer and no unit. The general quantity is the kind k; the individual quantity is\nIndividualQuantity o k R; and the number-and-unit form is produced by\nMetrologicalUnit.measure.* Quantity k R is not restricted by scale. The type can be formed at any kind,\nnominal included. What the scale restricts is the operations: addition and subtraction\nrequire a DifferenceKind, and products, quotients and reciprocals require the kind\nedges below. This matches quantity calculus in VIM 3 1.21, which excludes ordinal\nquantities, and in VIM 4 2CD 1.29, which applies to ratio and interval quantities.* The order is per kind. Quantity.instLE compares two quantities of the same kind\nonly; a comparison across kinds does not type-check. Quantity.instDecidableLE makes\nit decidable whenever the carrier's order is. The instance exists at every kind whose\ncarrier is ordered; whether the order is meaningful for the kind is the separate\ncondition AllowsOrder, which bounds require.* No counterpart: the number type. ISO 80000-1 and the VIM take \"number\" as given.\nCarrier and LawfulCarrier state what a number type must provide (a zero and an\naddition, then the laws of a commutative monoid). Nat and Int are lawful carriers.\nThe sources have no term for this, and it is where a vocabulary of numbers attaches to\na vocabulary of quantities.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Standards Reference — PKC beside ISO 80000-1 and the VIM","header":"34.5. Quantities, numbers, and the count","id":"/Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM/Quantities___-numbers___-and-the-count/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Quantities___-numbers___-and-the-count"}});